<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Belize</h1><p class="st-meta">1981 CE – present · capital Belmopan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Central America's only English-speaking country, independent since 1981, home to the world's second-longest barrier reef and a long border dispute with Guatemala.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/british-honduras">British Honduras</a> was renamed Belize in 1973, and despite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala's</a> claim to the territory it became independent on 21 September 1981 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-cadle-price">George Price</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-united-party">People's United Party</a>, with the British monarch as head of state. British troops stayed to guard it against <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-guatemala">Guatemala</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Power has alternated between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-united-party">People's United Party</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-democratic-party-belize">United Democratic Party</a>, under prime ministers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-esquivel">Manuel Esquivel</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/said-musa">Said Musa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dean-barrow">Dean Barrow</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johnny-briceno">Johnny Briceño</a>. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-guatemala">Guatemala</a> recognised Belize's independence in 1991, though not its borders, and the British garrison largely left in 1994. Tourism to the reef and Maya sites, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>, citrus, bananas and seafood support the economy. Its population mixes Kriol, Mestizo, Maya, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garifuna">Garifuna</a> and Mennonite communities.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>In 2018 and 2019 both <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-guatemala">Guatemala</a> and Belize voted to submit the territorial dispute to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-court-of-justice">International Court of Justice</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belmopan">Belmopan</a>, built after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hurricane-hattie">Hurricane Hattie</a>, is the capital, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belize-city">Belize City</a> is the largest town.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/belize.jpg" alt="Belize" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: Caleb Moore · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Belize.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1981 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Belmopan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>George Price, Said Musa, Dean Barrow</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism and Protestantism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English, Belizean Kriol and Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/belize">Belize</a></dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=2004&amp;focus=belize" aria-label="Belize on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2267 1537 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Belize lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2587 1793L2588 1800"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2588 1800h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2587 1793h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2587 1793h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Belmopan. <a href="/atlas?year=2004&amp;focus=belize">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
